+{"created_at": "2026-07-01T17:46:49.843216+00:00", "ocr_backend": "sglang", "ocr_endpoint_version": "modal-sglang-unlimited-ocr-v1", "ocr_model": "Unlimited-OCR", "ocr_model_version": "Unlimited-OCR", "ocr_text": "<PAGE><|det|>text [97, 68, 900, 163]<|/det|>token1.deposit{value: balanceSurplus}(); // Pool reserves are measured as balances, hence we can replenish it with token1 // by transferring directly token1.safeTransfer(stexInterface.pool(), balanceSurplus);\n<|det|>text [71, 197, 900, 239]<|/det|>This works because the token is a rebase token and so the Sovereign Pool uses the actual balance as reserve.\n<|det|>text [71, 253, 862, 296]<|/det|>However, this mechanism can be exploited to directly steal the entire surplus amount by leveraging the swap callback of the Sovereign Pool.\n<|det|>text [71, 308, 871, 376]<|/det|>When calling swap on the Sovereign Pool, the user has the option parameter isSwapCallback, which can be turned on to pay the tokenIn amount inside of the callback in _handleTokenInTransfersOnSwap:\n<|det|>text [97, 401, 812, 941]<|/det|>function _handleTokenInTransfersOnSwap(\n    bool isZeroToOne,\n    bool isSwapCallback,\n    IERC20 token,\n    uint256 amountInUsed,\n    uint256 effectiveFee,\n    bytes_callback _swapCallbackContext\n) private {\n    uint256 preBalance = token.balanceOf(sovereignVault);\n\n    if (isSwapCallback) {\nISovereignPoolSwapCallback(msg.sender).sovereignPoolSwapCallback(\n    address(token),\n    amountInUsed,\n    _swapCallbackContext\n    );\n} else {\n    token.saveTransferFrom(msg.sender, sovereignVault,\namountInUsed);\n}\n\nuint256 amountInReceived = token.balanceOf(sovereignVault) -\npreBalance;\n[...]\n}\n<|det|>page_number [910, 955, 935, 969]<|/det|>14\n", "page_image_sha256": "64650b541f7624a7c2e49dc3f66203bb1d8fc929b39ec7e444b04b17311000e4", "page_number": 14, "pdf_id": "pdf_002911_39a2c2ee71c9", "raw_record": {"code_blocks": [], "confidence": null, "created_at": "2026-07-01T17:46:49.843216+00:00", "layo