rawal, amountToken1, _recipient);\n<|det|>page_number [909, 955, 939, 970]<|/det|>22\n", "page_image_sha256": "569666f170d3d0182a431d8366a8b549c4903b1bd4c1c36db15b7a7cd94f2494", "page_number": 22, "pdf_id": "pdf_002911_39a2c2ee71c9", "raw_record": {"code_blocks": [], "confidence": null, "created_at": "2026-07-01T17:46:49.843216+00:00", "layout_blocks": [], "ocr_backend": "sglang", "ocr_endpoint_version": "modal-sglang-unlimited-ocr-v1", "ocr_model": "Unlimited-OCR", "ocr_model_version": "Unlimited-OCR", "ocr_settings": {"dpi": 300, "image_mode": "base", "mode": "pages", "ngram_size": 35, "ngram_window": 1024, "prompt": "Multi page parsing."}, "ocr_text": "<PAGE><|det|>text [70, 56, 904, 93]<|/det|>Moreover, it does not keep withdrawal sizes in mind. If a user withdraws 100 ETH, and the\n<|det|>text [70, 96, 905, 135]<|/det|>cumulativeAmountToken1ClaimableLPWithdrawalCheckpoint is X, they can claim the withdrawal at X + 100.\n<|det|>text [70, 145, 912, 185]<|/det|>The following logic allows smaller deposits to be bypass the priority queue and effectively gain feeless instant withdrawals of their stHYPE to HYPE.\n<|det|>text [97, 214, 866, 351]<|/det|>if (\n    cumulativeAmountToken1ClaimableLPWithdrawal\n    < request.cumulativeAmountToken1ClaimableLPWithdrawalCheckpoint +\nrequest.amountToken1\n) {\n    revert stHYPEWithdrawalModule__claim_cannotGetClaim();\n}\n<|det|>text [71, 386, 435, 406]<|/det|>Consider the following situation:\n<|det|>text [85, 415, 896, 474]<|/det|>1. User 1 withdraws 10 ETH\n(cumulativeAmountToken1ClaimableLPWithdrawalCheckpoint = X), so they can withdraw at X + 10.\n<|det|>text [82, 476, 890, 513]<|/det|>2. The burn request is filled and redeemed, the Overseer sends 10 ETH to the Withdrawal Module.\n<|det|>text [82, 516, 370, 533]<|/det|>3. User 2 withdraws 1 ETH.\n<|det|>text [82, 536, 520, 554]<|/det|>4. Update is now called and fills 10 ETH.\n<|det|>text [82, 557, 301, 574]<|/det|>5. User 2 can claim.\n<|det|>text [82, 577, 831, 616]<|/d